      <td>Upload your own EPUBs to the cloud, reading position syncs with phone/tablet</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>FBReader</strong></td>
      <td>OPDS catalogs and news</td>
      <td>Long-standing open-source reader with OPDS and online library support</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>If you are coming from a jailbroken Kindle or a Kobo and already use <a href="/blog/kindle-jailbreak-koreader-epub-2026/">KOReader</a>, you can install the Android build on the Palma and keep the exact same reading setup. For everyone else, start with NeoReader, then try one or two alternatives to find what fits.</p>

<h2 id="convert-formats">Converting Other Formats to EPUB for the Palma</h2>

<p>The Palma reads more than EPUB out of the box — NeoReader handles PDF, MOBI, AZW3, DJVU, CBR, and TXT. But for the best reading experience on a small 6.13-inch screen, <strong>EPUB is almost always the right format</strong>. Reflowable EPUB text adapts to any font size and margin you choose; fixed-layout PDFs force you to zoom and pan.</p>

<p>When a book arrives in a non-EPUB format, convert it before you side-load:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>PDF to EPUB</strong> — for text-based PDFs, conversion is clean and fast. Follow our <a href="/blog/pdf-to-epub-free-conversion-2026/">PDF-to-EPUB conversion guide</a> for the best free methods, and use this very site to convert files directly in your browser.</li>
  <li><strong>MOBI/AZW3 to EPUB</strong> — older Kindle files convert cleanly in Calibre or with online tools. Once converted, they behave like any other EPUB on the Palma.</li>
  <li><strong>DOCX to EPUB</strong> — manuscripts and documents convert well; Calibre's DOCX input filter is the most reliable free option.</li>
  <li><strong>Batch conversion</strong> — use <a href="/blog/calibre-cli-batch-conversion/">Calibre's command-line tools</a> to convert an entire folder of files at once, then side-load the results over USB.</li>
</ul>

<p>A quick tip: after conversion, run a metadata pass with <a href="/blog/calibre-edit-epub-metadata-2026/">Calibre's metadata editor</a> so titles, authors, and covers display correctly in your Palma library. Clean metadata makes a big difference when you are browsing hundreds of books on a small screen.</p>

<h2 id="organize">Organizing Your EPUB Library on the Palma</h2>

<p>With hundreds of books on a pocket-sized device, organization matters. Here is the setup that works best in practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Use folders on device storage</strong> — create <code>Books/Nonfiction</code>, <code>Books/Fiction</code>, and so on. NeoReader and ReadEra both index folder structure well.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean your metadata</strong> — a book with the right title, author, and cover is dramatically easier to find. Calibre's bulk metadata edit handles a whole library at once.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark finished books</strong> — most reader apps track reading progress automatically. In NeoReader, books you finish can be moved to a "Read" collection so your home shelf stays focused.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync reading position</strong> — if you also read on your phone, Google Play Books' "upload your own EPUB" feature keeps your place in sync across devices. Alternatively, KOReader offers cloud-synced progress via its OPDS and sync plugins.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you are starting fresh, build your library in Calibre first and use it as the single source of truth — then mirror folders to the Palma whenever you add books. That gives you a clean, searchable archive and a simple sync ritual.</p>

<h2 id="which-boox">Palma 2 vs Palma 2 Pro vs Picco: Which Boox Pocket Reader?</h2>

<p>Onyx Boox now sells three pocket-sized readers, and the right choice depends on what you value:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
